From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thibaut Lapierre <root@epheo.eu>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:26:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] ovn: shared gateway, the node IP as apiserver endpoint

OVN-Kubernetes runs in shared gateway mode with the node's NIC in br-ex,
OpenShift's topology: the image's configure-ovs moves the uplink and its
IP configuration into br-ex. Pod egress leaves through the gateway router
and the NIC, not the host routing table. --allow-no-uplink goes.

Forwarding follows OpenShift's default, ipForwarding Restricted: global
IPv4 and IPv6 forwarding off, --disable-forwarding, ovnkube enables it on
the interfaces it owns. With the NIC in br-ex, global forwarding would
route between the uplink and every other host interface.

The apiserver advertise address defaults to the node IP, as on OpenShift.
The virtual first address after the service CIDR only worked because it
was br-ex's only address: ovnkube took it as the gateway IP and hairpinned
it. Next to the node IP it is neither hairpinned for host-network clients
nor reachable from other nodes' pods, whose traffic now bypasses host
routes. So:
- nothing is added to an interface; workers lose the /32 alias and the
  routes to the control plane they needed for it.
- the node IP is served the service-network certificate, which pods
  validate against 10.43.0.1, never the external one: a subjectAltNames
  entry equal to the advertise address stays out of the external
  certificate, and its kubeconfig trusts the service-network signer.
- the advertise address always gets a kubeconfig, the node IP that
  worker-join-kubeconfig defaults to.
- validation checks a user-set advertise address on the host as before,
  not the node IP default.

mDNS listens on br-ex and node IP detection reads it: br-ex holds the node
IP. microshift-cleanup-data starts Open vSwitch again after the OVN wipe,
since stopping ovsdb-server takes ovs-vswitchd and the uplink down.
